-- A leaf is named in the notices of the build by its path, or by the column it comes from: the leaves of a jsonb column are more than its attributes, and leaf c is not
-- attribute c. The index below has three attributes and four leaves (j.a, j.b, id, t): the notice says id, the third leaf, not t, the third attribute, and reading the
-- fourth leaf as an attribute went past the descriptor (PostgreSQL 19 with assertions stopped the CREATE INDEX of agg_wildcard_key_cost on it).
CREATE EXTENSION pg_sift;
SET max_parallel_workers_per_gather = 0;
CREATE TABLE ln (j jsonb, id int PRIMARY KEY, t int) WITH (autovacuum_enabled = false);
INSERT INTO ln SELECT jsonb_build_object('a', i % 50, 'b', hashint4(i) & 1023), i, hashint4(i + 5) & 4095 FROM generate_series(1, 30000) i;
CREATE INDEX ln_ix ON ln USING sift_col (j, id, t) WITH (paths = 'j.a int4, j.b int4', order_array_columns = 'auto: id');
NOTICE:  sift_col index "ln_ix" installed two statement triggers on table "ln" and tracks its changes
DETAIL:  track_changes: AFTER UPDATE and AFTER DELETE, FOR EACH STATEMENT, with a transition table of the old rows; they tombstone the entries of the old key of an UPDATE that changes the key and of every DELETE, after a pass over the heap for the versions already dead. They are dropped with the index.
HINT:  WITH (track_changes = 'off') builds the index without them.
NOTICE:  sift_col index "ln_ix": order_array_columns = 'auto' keeps sorted arrays of id
DETAIL:  0.4 bytes a row for each whole array, 0.0 MB together. The segments are column format minor 38, which a library before 3.7 cannot read. The build chose the columns from the statistics of the table: 100,000 rows or more, 1,000 or more distinct values and a correlation with the heap order under 0.99 (the zones cannot skip units), at most 8 of them, and for a text column or a column of few values that the default formula pays (a table with no statistics is analyzed first). A column keeps the whole order when a scan with a bound in the middle of its values would read more cold blocks than the order's bytes are worth (64 kB of index a block), else the ends. The choice of each column: id: the whole order (a bound in the middle of its values reads many units). ORDER BY column [DESC] LIMIT n, and the same with a bound at the end of the order (column >= x ORDER BY column DESC LIMIT n), read n entries of an end, whatever the number of units, for n and an OFFSET up to the entries of the end; a range of a whole-order column (up to 16,384 rows) hands the heap the addresses between its bounds without reading a unit; a range in the middle of an ends-only column reads the units.
HINT:  To drop them, run ALTER INDEX ln_ix SET (order_array_columns = 'off') and REINDEX; order_array_columns = 'column' keeps the whole order of a column; set sift.col_format_minor to 14 to write none.
SELECT sift.col_verify('ln_ix');
 col_verify 
------------
 
(1 row)

DROP TABLE ln;
DROP EXTENSION pg_sift;
